course: history
lesson: 72
title: Why some places grew rich
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Look at a map at night from space and the bright spots sit on coasts and rivers: water gave drinking water, fish and the cheapest road there was, so goods and people gathered there.
explain: In Europa Universalis, Venice grows fat on its trade node and Spain fills its treasury from American silver mines; the game even makes prices rise, called inflation, when you dig too much gold, and real history did something similar.
explain: Real wealth came from many things at once: geography, trade, skills, schools, good laws, inventions and sometimes taking from others; historians still argue which mattered most, and the answer is different for each place.
explain: Rich places also change: China and India were the biggest economies for centuries, Europe pulled ahead in the 1800s, and Singapore and South Korea went from poor to rich within one lifetime.
explain: So ask not only «what did they have?» but «what did they do with it?»
fact: Most old cities grew beside rivers, lakes or seas, which gave water, fish and cheap transport for goods.
fact: In «Guns, Germs and Steel» (1997) Jared Diamond argued that geography, plants and animals gave some regions an early lead; many historians reply that he left out people's choices and politics, so the idea is contested.
fact: Venice had little farmland but grew rich from trade between Europe and the eastern Mediterranean, from about 1000 to 1500.
fact: Silver from Potosí, in today's Bolivia, flowed to Spain from the 1500s; much was spent on wars, prices rose, Spain's kings ran out of money several times, and much of the silver ended up in China, which wanted silver.
fact: In the 1600s the small Netherlands grew rich through trade and banking; in Amsterdam people bought and sold shares, small parts of the Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, which gave them a piece of its profits.
fact: Historians argue why factories started in Britain in the 1700s: reasons given include cheap coal, high wages that made machines worth building, inventors, and money from trade and colonies.
fact: By some estimates China and India together made about half of the world's goods around 1700; Europe pulled ahead in the 1800s.
fact: Singapore became independent in 1965 as a poor port; with trade, schools and clean government it became one of the richest places in the world, and South Korea, poorer than many countries in the 1950s, also grew rich.
fact: Norway puts most of its oil money into a huge fund for the future; some other oil countries did not grow rich for most people, which some economists call a «resource curse», an idea that is debated.
fact: After 1868 Japan studied factories, schools and laws from other countries and built its own versions, becoming an industrial power within decades.
fact: Some countries grew rich partly by taking land, goods and labour from colonies and from enslaved people; how much this explains today's wealth is still debated by historians.
exercise: Why are so many cities next to rivers or seas? | Water gave drinking water, fish and cheap transport | Rivers keep cities warm in winter, like a big heater | Kings liked looking at the water from their palaces | Cities were built at random, and rivers came later
exercise: What did Jared Diamond argue in «Guns, Germs and Steel»? | Geography, plants and animals gave some regions an early lead | Some peoples are simply born cleverer than other peoples | Only kings decide which countries get rich and which stay poor | Wealth comes only from gold mines and nothing else
exercise: What do many historians say is missing from Diamond's idea? | People's choices and politics | Any mention of geography or maps | Any mention of farming or animals | The names of the oceans and the seas
exercise: How did Venice grow rich with so little farmland? | By trade between Europe and the eastern Mediterranean | By growing wheat on great rafts floating on its canals | By mining gold from deep under the lagoon | By conquering China and taking its silk
exercise: Where did much of Spain's silver come from? | Potosí, in today's Bolivia | The high mountains of Scotland | The middle of the Sahara desert | Old silver mines deep under Madrid
exercise: Why did American silver not keep Spain rich for ever? | Much was spent on wars, prices rose and much flowed on to China | The silver turned out to be fake, painted lead from the mines | Spain gave all of it away to the poor of Europe and Africa | Pirates stole every single ship that ever sailed with silver on it
exercise: Where did much of the world's silver end up in the 1500s and 1600s? | In China | At the bottom of the sea | In Ireland | In the Arctic
exercise: What was traded in Amsterdam from the 1600s? | Shares of the Dutch East India Company | Coins for the first video arcade games | Shares of new steam railway companies | Tickets for the Olympic Games
exercise: Which of these is a reason historians give for factories starting in Britain? | Cheap coal and high wages that made machines worth building | Britain had the most sunshine in Europe, so it grew the most | Britain had no trade at all, so it had to make everything | A single inventor did it all alone in one small workshop
exercise: Around 1700, which two countries together made about half the world's goods, by some estimates? | China and India | Britain and France | Spain and Portugal | The USA and Canada
exercise: What happened to Singapore after 1965? | It went from a poor port to one of the richest places | It stayed poor because it is small and has no land | It became a part of China and its biggest port | It was abandoned by its people
exercise: What does Norway do with most of its oil money? | Saves it in a huge fund for the future | Spends it all in one single year on parties | Burns it as fuel in the winter | Gives it to oil companies abroad
exercise: How did Japan grow fast after 1868? | By studying other countries and building its own versions | By closing itself off from the world and trading with nobody | By finding a huge gold mine under Mount Fuji | By closing all its schools
exercise: Did some countries grow rich partly by taking from others? | Yes, through colonies and slavery, though how much is debated | No, never once in the whole of history, not even a little | Yes, and it is the only reason any country ever got rich | No, colonies always made empires poorer
exercise: What is the «resource curse»? | The debated idea that oil or minerals do not always make most people richer | A spell that old miners believed was cast on deep gold mines by angry mountain spirits | A law that stops any country from selling its oil abroad | The rule that every country with oil must stay poor
test: Noah's dad: "This Diamond bloke reckons geography explains everything, case closed." What would a careful historian say? | Geography matters a lot, but choices and politics matter too, and it is debated | Your dad's right: geography explains every single thing, so the case really is closed | Geography explains nothing at all about history | Only the weather decides history, not maps
test: In Europa Universalis, Carlos piles up gold from his mines and his inflation shoots up. His dad says, "Lowkey, that's what happened to real Spain." ("Lowkey" means sort of, quietly.) Is that fair? | Yes, a flood of American silver helped push prices up in Spain | No, more silver always makes everyone richer, so the game is wrong | No, Spain never had any silver, only gold | Yes, but only in the game, never in real life
test: Sofia, in Venice: "How'd a city sat on water get so minted?" ("Minted" means rich.) What's the answer? | Venice was a trading hub between Europe and the eastern Mediterranean | Venice had the biggest farms in Italy | Venice found oil under the canals and sold it all over the world | Venice was given money by the Pope every year
test: Liam's tour guide says the tall, thin Amsterdam houses show how precious canal-side land was. Why was land there so wanted? | The city was a busy, rich trading centre with little room by the canals | Dutch people liked climbing stairs for fun and exercise | Houses were built thin so that the storms from the sea would pass by | Nobody wanted to live near the canals
test: Mei: "China and India were the richest in the old days, my teacher said." Her mate: "Cap!" ("Cap" means a lie.) Who's right? | Mei: by some estimates they made about half the world's goods around 1700 | Her mate: Europe was always the richest part of the world, by every estimate | Neither: Africa made nearly everything | Nobody can ever know anything about the past
test: Tom's mate says factories started in Britain because "Brits are just built different, innit." What's fairer? | Historians point to coal, wages, inventors and trade money, and still argue | British people are born cleverer | Factories started in Britain by pure luck alone, and nobody can say more than that | Factories started in China in 1700
test: Ethan's gran: "Back in my day Singapore was a sleepy little port." Ethan: "And now it's rich? How?" | Trade, good schools and clean government helped it grow fast | It found the biggest gold mine on Earth | It became rich by winning a big war against Japan and taking its factories | It is still a sleepy little port
test: Ola from Norway: "We've got oil, but we save the dosh." ("Dosh" means money.) What would happen if a country spent all its oil money at once? | It could have little left when the oil runs out | It would stay rich for ever, automatically, whatever it did | The oil under the sea would just refill itself | It would have to stop selling oil abroad
test: Yuki's friend: "Japan got rich by copying? That's sus." ("Sus" means suspicious.) Fairest reply? | Learning from others and building your own versions is smart, not cheating | Japan stole everything and built nothing | Japan never learned from anyone at all | Copying is the only way any country has ever grown, so everyone copied everything
test: Amir asks, "Did some countries get rich by nicking stuff off others?" ("Nicking" means stealing.) Best answer? | Some did, through colonies and slavery, and historians debate how much it explains | No country ever took anything from another | Yes, and it is the only reason any country is rich, so historians have nothing left to argue about | Only small countries ever did that
test: Which is the wisest summary of why places grow rich? | Many causes together: geography, trade, skills, laws, and sometimes taking from others | Only gold and silver, because the countries with the most mines always end up the richest | Only the weather | Only the cleverness of one king
exam: Identify the principal source of the prosperity of Venice between approximately the years one thousand and fifteen hundred. | Commerce between Europe and the eastern Mediterranean | Extensive cultivation of grain upon the islands of the lagoon | The extraction of gold from beneath the city | Tribute paid by the Chinese emperors
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequences for Spain of the silver of Potosí? | Much was expended upon wars, prices rose, and a great part passed onward to China | It ensured permanent and unrivalled prosperity for Spain | It was never transported out of the Americas | It was wholly distributed among the Spanish peasantry
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the thesis advanced in «Guns, Germs and Steel». | It ascribes early advantages to geography, plants and animals, and is contested by many historians | It is accepted without reservation by all historians and has never been seriously disputed by any scholar | It ascribes wealth solely to the decisions of monarchs | It denies that geography has any influence
exam: Ascertain the manner in which Japan achieved industrialisation subsequent to eighteen hundred and sixty-eight. | By studying foreign institutions and constructing its own versions of them | By the complete exclusion of all foreign contact and the prohibition of trade | By the discovery of extensive silver deposits | By the abolition of all schools
